\\begin\{abstract\}\
\\thispagestyle\{empty\}\
\
While a large canon of psychological and management literature claims a positive impact of higher education on leadership, few studies quantitatively link educational attainment to the financial outcomes for organizations. This paper seeks to unravel whether CEO educational attainment (particularly in the form of advanced degrees) can explain the financial performance of high technology companies.\
\
A hand-selected, merged dataset is built including financial reporting data from 151 firms (biotech, hardware, and software) from 2015 to 2025. For each firm-year observation, the operating CEO and five educational attainment variables (undergraduate, master's, MBA, PHD, and MD) are added. Financial, CEO-specific, and compensation-related controls are included to address education-adjacent omitted variable bias.\
\
Across two separate specifications, CEO higher educational attainment shows a significant relationship with firm performance (measured by normalized return on assets) for biotech and hardware \\& semiconductor firms. Biotech CEOs with PHDs are associated with stronger ROA performance, while Biotech CEOs with MDs work at companies that underperform their peers. For semiconductor and hardware firms, CEOs with PHDs are associated with stronger ROA performance, while CEOs with MBAs are associated with companies that perform poorly.\
\
These observational results tie strongly to psychological research suggesting that individuals with higher "integrative complexity" perform better in solving complex problems and leading. The findings of this thesis present initial support for the notion that successful technology leadership, controlling for firm characteristics and compensation incentives, requires an education-derived capacity for high complexity information.\
\
