\\section\{Introduction\}\
Since the 1960s, psychological research has ratified the value of a strong education. At the individual level, educational attainment leads to improved decision making, cognitive performance, and leadership. Contemporary population views align; more than 90\\% of U.S. citizens with an undergraduate degree believe that higher education pays off in the long term value (Pew Research Center, 2014).\
\
High Technology companies typically agree with psychologists and population sentiment. An increasing number of top employers require employees to have degrees from selective and prestigious undergraduate institutions. For higher level management and executive roles, a strong educational background is a baseline expectation (Ellis, 2026).\
\
Despite this implicit assumption \'97 that well-educated  leaders should drive more successful innovation \'97 few studies examine the data-driven relationship between leadership educational attainment and technology firm performance. While some strands of management literature gravitate to specific examples of well-educated CEOs or operators turning R\\&D-intensive firms around, these studies do not provide formalized quantitative results. Outside of Knowledge-Based sectors, a handful of studies have identified the value of management education in specific fields, such as the improved income performance of banks led by CEOs with MBAs (King et al., 2016). The lack of studies unpacking the role leadership education plays in innovation-driven firms constitutes the core motivation of this paper.\
\
Using financial data from the Wharton Research Data Services (WRDS), this research paper seeks to explore the following question \'97 \\textit\{For R\\&D-intensive, innovation-driven companies, how do the educational backgrounds of company CEOs (both undergraduate and specified advanced degrees) impact the performance of the underlying companies?\} Throughout the forthcoming analysis, the initial alternative hypothesis will be that advanced degrees (PHDs, medical doctorates) lead to stronger financial performance (measured as an adjustment of return on assets).\
\
The literature review that follows will discuss  previous psychology research and management papers that motivate the potential importance of educational attainment in this analysis. It will also detail the behavioral finance papers providing the baseline of our methodological design. The Dataset overview will provide an explanation of the modified WRDS dataset and its final construction. The Methodology section will outline the baseline regressions utilized to explain the impact of educational attainment and provide a comprehensive overview of key control variables. Finally, initial results will be shared alongside a discussion of findings and what they may imply for the industry groups of companies being analyzed.\
\
