\\section\{Literature Review\}\
\\subsection\{Psychology and Management Literature on Individual Cognition\}\
In the 1960s, psychological researchers began publishing explicit findings about the individual impact of education. In his seminal work on Human Capital, Gary Becker assessed "education" and "on-the-job training" in tandem, identifying them as particularly strong drivers of earnings and security of employment. Becker's work treats education in the manner of an accountant analyzing a company's capital investment, calculating the financial returns on investment for various kinds of degrees (Becker, 1964). Becker's work not only cemented the academic and public perception of education's role in individual success, but also sketched the first analogy of education as a financial independent variable (motivating its role in the management and financial papers to eventually follow).\
\
In the few years following Becker's first publication, a number of theorists expanded on his work, providing new theories about the role of complex cognition in problem-solving. Princeton researchers Marvin Karlins et. al tested the value of individual capacities for complex information; in a problem-solving task, individuals that were classified as having more "integrative complexity" asked more questions and performed better in learning tasks. They also exhibited stronger performance when presented with tasks characterized by less structure and required action (Karlins et al., 1967). Although an early step in the development of psychological frameworks around education, the concept of "integrative complexity" provides a compelling basis for the hypothesis of this paper. Given that the CEOs of R\\&D-heavy companies are likely to face complex, unstructured problems, it is rational to hypothesize that achieving a higher education would improve their ability to lead effectively.\
\
Eventually, studies of management and educational psychology directly overlapped. Philip Mason and Donald Hambrick proposed Upper Echelons Theory, which draws a relationship between executive teams and two measures of performance: strategic choices and performance levels. After observing top performing management teams, they created a model tying cognitive ability to a number of background characteristics, including age, experience, financial background, and education (Hambrick \\& Mason, 1984). These findings present one of the first fundamental overlaps between the psychological characterization of "cognitive ability" and corporate leadership acumen. Even more importantly, they acknowledge the importance of control variables in the context of education (age, tenure, background information) and the difficulty of drawing appropriate causality; just as strong education could yield impressive cognitive ability, impressive cognitive ability might have led a company's leader to attain a strong education in the first place.\
\
The proposition that basic profile details could predict a leader's ability to execute effective strategy birthed a number of follow-ups on Upper Echelons theory. Professors Marianne Bertrand and Antoinette Schoar identify statistically significant patterns in decision-making based solely on experience and education. For older, higher tenure executives, they demonstrate that leaders execute more conservative management decisions than younger managers. With regards to education, they find that managers with MBA degrees tend to enact more aggressive firm strategies (Bertrand \\& Schoar, 2003). This explicit articulation of management style ties to the "integrative complexity" described in Marvin Karlins' work, suggesting for this body of research that different degrees may lead to executives operating with differences in approach (and resultantly, differences in performance).\
\
The choice of this research methodology to concentrate on CEO education in particular originates from a handful of papers that scrutinize CEO education and firm performance. For example, Professors Deepak Datta and Nandini Rajagopalan assess the performance of successor CEOs (joining after a CEO leaves their position) in capital investment, advertising intensity, and board power. They find that education is a statistically significant predictor of CEO performance in these categories for successor CEOs (Datta \\& Rajagopalan, 1998). Datta and Rajagopalan's work not only validates the CEO as a core individual responsible for company-wide strategic decision making, but also motivates this study's scrutiny of potential corruption of transition years for the CEOs included in the analysis.\
\
While the psychological groundwork and resulting follow-on studies provide a logical explanation for the variable selection, baseline design, and working hypothesis of this research, drawing a clear relationship requires better access to firm-specific controls and proper addressing of endogeneity concerns. To understand how this strand of research might properly account for CEO education, assessing the behavioral finance literature and its implications for measuring firm performance are detailed in the following sections.\
\
\\subsection\{Financial Literature and Methodological Groundwork\}\
A number of core behavioral finance studies utilize publicly listed stock returns as a core dependent variable for their analysis. These studies consider a plethora of quantitative indicators, including time-of-year effects (Chen \\& Singal, 2004), volatility (Ang et al., 2009), and country-of-trade (Froot, 1999), illustrating how publicly available information about these variables can impact the market pricing of the securities in question. However, the majority of these papers utilize these variables to understand how markets behave in the context of such information, but not how firms themselves actually perform. The implication is that even when firm performance does not change, market pricing can respond to changes in available information and generate "financial" returns for investors.\
\
When seeking strategically honest, sentiment-agnostic measures of firm performance, seminal papers from the financial literature routinely select return on assets (ROA) as a successful tool across different independent variables, industry categories, and geographies of interest. Computed by dividing the net income of a company by the value of its total assets, the measure allows for comparison between different firms regardless of their scale, industry, or operating geography (Barber \\& Lyon, 1996). Furthermore, ROA is resistant to the market volatility created by sentiment and information-driven investors.\
\
The literature precedents for constructing education variables inform the inclusion of binary indicators and the value of capturing "school prestige." In their examination of mutual fund managers, Professors Judith Chevalier and Glen Ellison assess how the average composite SAT score of an individual's undergraduate school, MBA completion status, and age ultimately affect the performance of the fund they manage. Their main conclusion outlines that managers who attended higher-SAT undergraduate institutions have stronger risk-adjusted returns for their portfolios (Chevalier \\& Ellison, 2002). Ellison and Chevalier's work not only exemplifies the value of constructing degree-type binary variables (e.g. MBA, yes or no), but also the importance of capturing some measure of ranking or quality as a potential driver of cognitive success.\
\
This study's methodology draws primarily from a CEO education project that was completed by Professors Timothy King, Abhishek Srivastav, and Jonathan Williams. Focusing on the U.S. banking sector, their paper constructs a hand-built dataset capturing CEO education (both attainment percentages and quality) and ROA performance observations from 1992 to 2011. They define financial controls (including stock pricing volatility, macroeconomic conditions, and bank size) alongside CEO-specific controls (including compensation and experience) in order to reduce the potential impact of financial and external factors. Across multiple robustness checks and endogeneity attempts, they demonstrate that bank CEOs with MBAs are associated with stronger ROA performance (King et al., 2016).\
\
This banking-specific analysis differentiates itself from preceding papers because of its narrow industry focus. By concentrating on a specific sector, hyper-localized educational effects take stronger hold in the data; MBA-earning CEOs may not affect all industries equally (with effects cancelling out or neutralizing positive findings), but they do specifically provide strong performance value for banking executives. This confirms a R\\&D specific lens for this paper; with the prevalence of high development cost, public technology companies in the 2010s, isolating a subset of innovation-oriented industries may lead to more significant findings than running the same testing for an economy-wide sample.\
\
Treating ROA and firm-specific controls as a panel dataset also constitutes a valuable lesson from King et al. By choosing a specific set of fiscal year observations over 19 years, they capture different iterations of CEO tenure, tie ROA performance with year-by-year CEO profiles, and include more samples in their final dataset.\
\
Finally, their study of banking CEO education correctly humbles the aspirations of this canon of research. Even with positive results and endogeneity checks, their findings are fundamentally limited by the lingering possibility of reverse causality. The aforementioned conundrum remains; just as a well-educated CEO might build out a high ROA company, a high ROA company might benefit from the flexibility to hire a well-educated CEO.\
\
The forthcoming section details the construction of a comparable dataset for hand-selected technology companies from 2015-2025 using WRDS querying and data concision. The methodology section will delve into the core approach utilized by King et al. and outline the adjustments made for a technology-oriented dataset. Finally, the Results and Discussion sections will outline initial regression results and compare them to the banking-specific findings described above.\
\
