\\subsection\{Compustat\}\
\
The North American Compustat database provides individual data points for U.S. and Canadian companies from their financial statements. The dataset extends from 1950 to the time of this paper's writing, providing access to key information reported by publicly traded firms in each year (S\\&P Global Market Intelligence, 2026).\
\
One of the primary benefits of the Compustat datset is the inclusion of firm-specific SIC (standard industrial classification) codes. SIC codes enable granular selection of peer groups of firms with similar operating practices, target markets, and product lines. Given this paper's concentration on high-R\\&D, technology-oriented firms, SIC codes were the filtration mechanism for selecting relevant U.S. companies that met the industry focus criteria. For a total of 10 selected SIC codes, companies were classified under three categories: Semiconductors and Hardware, Biotech, and Software. Table 1 outlines the 10 SIC codes, their corresponding sub-industry, and their high-level industry classification for the purposes of the forthcoming dataset construction (OSHA, 2026):\
