WIth the above SIC codes, all U.S. companies falling under the above three industry classifications are withdrawn (name and ticker). This provides the basis for extracting financial data for each of the respective firms through Compustat. A few hand-selected companies are removed from the dataset (rationale detailed in Appendix A).\
\
Compustat provides all core financial figures for this study's selected companies. For annual fiscal years from 2015 to 2025, financial figures include outstanding shares, cost of shares at end of fiscal year, total equity value, total asset value, total debt, retained earnings, net income, and research and development (R\\&D) expenditure. Calculations are performed on these figures in order to construct dependent variables and controls for eventual analysis (for example, ROA will be computed as the net income divided by total asset value). Once collected, these financial figures constitute the baseline for this study's panel data. The comprehensive list of collected financial figures are detailed in \\textit\{Appendix B: Collected Compustat Financial Data\}.\
\
Compustat also provides the strongest-available executive compensation data in WRDS through a dataset called ExecuComp. Execucomp provides data on each selected public company's top five executive officers (when available) (WRDS, 2026). Given the importance of including CEO-specific compensation controls (with the potential to impact management incentives), this panel dataset also leverages CEO annual compensation figures (total compensation, cash base salary, cash bonus, equity bonus, unexercisable/unexercised options, exercisable/unexercised options). Because core CEO educational data is taken from BoardEx (see section \\textit\{3.2 Boardex\}), a WRDS data bridge is performed between BoardEx CEO identification numbers and Execucomp that allows for panel data matching. Collected Execucomp variables are detailed in \\textit\{Appendix C: Collected Execucomp Compensation Data\}.\
\
\\subsection\{BoardEx\}\
\
Available through WRDS, BoardEx is an aggregator of executive data, detailing profiles of key leadership personnel at a large list of publicly traded U.S. firms. In addition to providing lists of managers and their positions at respective companies, BoardEx provides access to employment history, birth information, and educational data that are the critical makeup of this paper's independent variables and CEO-specific controls.\
\
Equipped with a list of industry-relevant firms from Compustat, BoardEx must be queried in order to extract BoardEx-specific company identification numbers. Filtering data from 2015 to 2025, these company identification values yield a gross list of company executives for the dates and securities requested. For each of those executives, basic employment information (start date, position, seniority) and personal information (name, date of birth, state inhabited) are provided. \
\
To isolate the dataset to CEO-only entries, language processing filters and seniority filters (executive CEOs only) are applied through a python scrypt to isolate Chief Executive Officers from their peers. The results is a consolidated list of the specific CEOs that served at the companies of interest from the start of 2015 to the end of 2025.\
\
A separate BoardEx query is then performed on the specific CEO identification numbers. This query extracts educational data for each of the CEOs (degree, institution, date of award, etc.). For a given CEO, multiple rows may appear, with each row representing a separate degree awarded. \
\
Valuable CEO-specific controls are also available through BoardEx and are extracted in the querying process. These include CEO age, tenure, and gender.\
\
A comprehensive list of extracted BoardEx variables used in the construction of this paper's panel dataset are included in \\textit\{Appendix D: Collected BoardEx Variables\}\
\
\\subsection\{Center for Research in Security Prices\}\
Available through WRDS, the Center for Research in Security Prices (CRSP) provides benchmark accuracy in market data for the United States, including stock performance of publicly listed firms. While this study prioritizes the use of ROA over stock performance as an indicator of financial performance, including CRSP stock trading data still provides useful controls for the panel data analysis. As a result, closing stock data for the selected tickers are extracted from CRSP for eventual merging with our panel dataset.\
\
\\subsection\{Data Consolidation and Descriptive Statistics\}\
\
With multiple queries performed across the three datasets (Compustat, BoardEx, CRSP), merging panel data requires careful attention to correct linkage of CEO, firm, and year related attributes. For example, when merging CEO education data with CEO control data, data merging occurs on the basis of the CEO BoardEx identification number. When merging CEO education and control data with Execucomp compensation data, merging occurs based on the bridge that links Execucomp CEO identification numbers to BoardEx CEO identification numbers. When merging occurs between the Compustat financial data and CEO financial data, merging must occur across the correct CEO, company identification, and year values.\
\
A number of data processing calculations are performed. As mentioned above, some calculations constitute basic column generation (ROA is generated as net income divided by total assets). The totality of these basic variable constructions are detailed in Appendix E. However, for the educational independent variables, this construction process is more complex.\
\
Initially, CEO education fields are provided as separate rows, with each row representing a degree awarded. To flatten a CEO observation into one row, each CEO identification number is assigned a binary variable for each of five forms of educational attainment (undergraduate, master's, MBA, PHD, and MD). If a CEO has a row corresponding to one of the degree types, the degree type is marked with a completion indicator.\
\
Prestige of each degree is captured using a top 20 binary variable for each degree type. Gathering the top 20 U.S. universities from U.S. News and World reporting (U.S. News \\& World Report, 2026), a language processing scrypt is applied to each "awarding institution" string. If a school falls under the top 20 institution list, a binary variable is applied. Top 20 binary variables are generated for each of the degree types present in the CEO row (top 20 undergraduate school, top 20 master's school, etc.). \
\
The result of having 10 binary variables representing educational attainment is that there are 10 closely correlated educational variables. To avoid multicollinearity, this paper mirrors the solution provided by King et al. in their banking CEO analysis (King et al., 2016). Factor analysis is performed in order to address multicollinearity, yielding a set of 5 factors that receive their attribution from the corresponding 5 degree types present in the underlying analysis. The attribution of each of the 10 binary variables to these factors for the baseline dataset (excluding compensation data) is outlined in \\textit\{Table 2\} below:\
