\\subsection\{Baseline and Compensation Datasets\}\
\
After merging flattened and factor-analyzed educational data with the existing merged panel dataset, a preliminary dataset is completed. The core dataset for this paper's analysis excludes executive compensation data (as more than 30\\% of CEO compensation figures are unavailable). Thus, the primary analysis (and core results) are separated from the data-constrained results that include executive compensation.\
\
The consolidated baseline dataset has 943 observation rows (2015 to 2025) and 151 unique high R\\&D firms, which is in the range of King et al.'s 1,032 firm-year observations for 149 banks. In total, there is a relatively even distribution between the three industry categories, with 57 biotech firms, 55 semiconductor and hardware firms, and 39 software firms included in the sample. There are 15 included variables across key analysis values, financial controls, and CEO-specific controls. Baseline dataset descriptive statistics are provided in Appendix G.\
\
The consolidated compensation dataset merges Execucomp compensation data onto the baseline dataset. Because Execucomp does not comprehensively cover compensation for all 154 CEOs in the baseline dataset, a number of panel data observations are omitted in the construction of a compensation-inclusive panel dataset. In total, there are 601 total observations for 37 unique biotech firms, 38 unique semiconductor and hardware firms, and 19 unique software firms. The compensation-inclusive dataset descriptive statistics are provided in Appendix H.\
\
In the forthcoming methodology section, the variables collected above are discussed in relation to the King et al. approach to evaluating educational attainment. \
