\\section\{Methodology\}\
The core methodological approach of this thesis derives from King et al. and their study of CEO education for U.S. publicly listed banks. Throughout the Methodology section, variables drawn from their methodology are attributed to the bank-CEO approach, while supplemental or modified variables are acknowledged. While the core regression setup mirrors their methodology, a number of variable modifications are imposed because of differences in available data and the fundamental differences of evaluating innovation-oriented firms.\
\
\\subsection\{Dependent Variable: Return on Assets\}\
\
As mentioned in the literature review of this paper, return on assets (ROA) constitutes a strong dependent variable because of its proximity to financial fundamentals and insensitivity to shifts in market sentiment. For the Compustat-derived panel dataset, raw ROA is calculated as the division of net income by value of total assets for each firm in each year of observation.\
\
In their bank CEO study, King et al. utilize a mean-adjusted measure of ROA, subtracting the industry-year mean from each observation of ROA. This dataset replicates the normalization, which should capture positive or negative performance in comparison to peers rather than raw values for ROA.\
\
\\subsection\{Independent Variable: Educational Attainment\}\
The above Data Consolidation and Descriptive Statistics Section (3.4) explicitly details the construction of education factors. Factor analysis allows for the inclusion of school rankings (binary variables for top 20 U.S. colleges) alongside degree fulfillment binary variables, eliminating multicollinearity from education data while still capturing the weighting of the different degrees.\
\
Thus, the independent variables in this analysis are factor variables for undergraduate, master's, MBA, PHD, and MD degree fulfillment. Each of these factors is scaled appropriately based on whether or not the respective degree was achieved at a top 20 school in the United States. \
\
\\subsection\{Control Variables\}\
\\subsubsection*\{Financial Controls\}\
\
Financial controls are critical in order to capture other potential drivers of profitability (and correspondingly, ROA). This prevents the misinterpretation of positive coefficients on CEO educational factors due to underlying financial differences in the firms being analyzed.\
\
King et al. control for firm size, equity capital, charter value, deposits, volatility, and retained earnings. Firm (log of total assets) can have a potential impact on economies of scale and impact profitability, so it is included in the financial control set. Equity capital (defined as equity value divided by asset value) and charter value (market-to-book value of equity) are potential drivers or correlates with profitability, and are also included. For this analysis, bank deposits are omitted because technology companies do not have substantial customer deposits that can be measured against assets. Average annual volatility (CRSP-derived) and retained earnings are included because of their potential connections to management risk taking and behavior.\
\
In lieu of bank deposits, values for R\\&D expenditure are widely available throughout the WRDS panel dataset. R\\&D scaled as a proportion of total assets is added to the panel dataset, allowing the regression to capture differences in innovation expenditure between different firms. To address the profitability impact of any macroeconomic or industry-wide shifts, a measure for annual U.S. GDP growth (2015 to 2025) is also added to the set of financial controls.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{CEO-specific Controls\}\
\
CEO-specific controls are included in the regression dataset in order to address available attributes of CEOs that may impact decision-making independent of their educational attainment. Age and gender are the main attributes available through BoardEx. Effects in tandem with Bertrand and Schoar's findings on management "style" (that older CEOs display conservative characteristic) may be mitigated by including controls for age (Bertrand \\& Schoar, 2003). While our literature review does not explicitly address gender-related differences in management decision-making, the availability of CEO gender in the BoardEx dataset motivates its inclusion as an extra control for robustness.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{Compensation Controls\}\
\
Compensation controls are separated from CEO-specific controls because of their explicit impact on the panel dataset. Because of missing compensation data for a number of CEOs in the dataset, inclusion of non-missing compensation data reduces the panel data observations from 943 observations (compensation not included) to 601 observations (compensation included). Thus, results of any regression including compensation controls must be scrutinized for the arbitrary reduction of the dataset and the reduced number of observations.\
\
There are four compensation controls included in the compensation regression. The percentage of equity compensation measures equity compensation for a given year against the entirety of a CEO's compensation figure for a given year, capturing how much of the CEO's pay is tied to firm performance. The delta proxy represents the proportion of unexercised options to total compensation, thereby capturing the level of sensitivity the CEO exhibits to movements in the price of the company's listed stocks. Both of these measures capture the degree of "risk" a CEO faces with their compensation and their implied propensity to pursue strategic risk taking. The other two measurements \'97 log of total compensation and percentage of total compensation in cash \'97 capture control variables for the more stable components of CEO compensation.\
\
\\subsection\{Combined Variables and Regression Specification\}\
\
Having gathered dependent, independent, and control variables in two unified sets of panel data, a baseline regression can be performed across firms from all three industry categories (biotech, semiconductors and hardware, and software). Our baseline specification is as follows:\
\\begin\{center\}\
    $\\text\{Firm Performance\}_\{i, t+1\} = \\beta _0 + \\beta _1(\\text\{UG Factor\}) + ... + \\beta _5(\\text\{MD Factor\}) + \\beta _6(\\text\{Controls\}) + \\text\{Firm\}_i + \\text\{Year\}_t + \\epsilon_\{i, t\}$\
\\end\{center\}\
The compensation-included specification is akin to the above, but assumes inclusion of CEO compensation controls in the controls variables of the regression. The primary variables of interest are the five factors (one for each educational qualification). The control variables discussed above are included under the 6th coefficient, and the Firm and Year fields capture firm and year fixed effects. \
\
The following section details the initial results of running the above regression.\
\
