\\section\{Discussion of Findings\}\
\\subsection\{Baseline Specification: Overall and Industry-Specific Findings\}\
\\subsubsection*\{Overall Baseline\}\
Baseline regressions compute results with the most robust dataset constructed from this paper's data queries. Across all three industries, there are 943 observations (in line with King et al.'s sampling). There are 340 biotech-specific observations, 389 Semiconductor and Hardware observations, and 214 software observations, with 47, 55, and 39 unique firms, respectively.\
\
When executed on the full panel dataset, no singular educational factor pulls a coefficient with a statistically significant, non-zero result. Firm size and retained earnings are significant at the 10\\% level, which justify their inclusion in the control set; firm size is a partial reciprocal of ROA and justifies the negative coefficient, while retained earnings have a logically positive coefficient because of their adjacency to net income. Charter value, equity capital, and R\\&D intensity all exhibit significance at the 1\\% level. Representing higher market premiums and lower utilization of debt, charter value and equity capital exhibit commensurate positive coefficients. The negative coefficient on R\\&D intensity likely draws from the situations where firms investing heavily in research and development are income-constrained.\
\
There are a handful of potential explanations for the lack of significant educational attainment results at the panel-wide regression level. For one, it is a possibility that an industry-agnostic, albeit industry focused, panel dataset provides too much diversification. This would mean that across the different characteristics and capital structures of the observed 151 firms, no particular degree type was consistently significant enough across the dataset. Another more ambitious explanation is the balancing of different effects across the three different sub industries. There is some data-driven basis for this explanation; for four out of the five factors (excluding the Master's Factor), the three sub-industry coefficients include both positive and negative values. While generally not significant in the baseline specification, this potentially indicates that slight negative or positive effects potentially cancel out when combining multiple sub-industries. Further commentary on this phenomenon occurs in the forthcoming \\textit\{Compensation Results\} section. Full regression results for the baseline, panel-wide dataset are detailed in Appendix I.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{Biotech Baseline\}\
Biotech constitutes the strongest result pool from the baseline specification and yields one of the core findings of this paper's entire analysis. Significant at the 95\\% level, the PHD factor (which captures the attainment of a PHD degree) explains a significant and positive impact on ROA. Within the locality of biotech firms, this strengthens this paper's core operating hypothesis; an advanced degree (i.e. a PHD) for a biotech CEO potentially explains positive financial performance when controlling for CEO-specific and firm-wide controls. \
\
One potential limitation of this significant coefficient on the PHD factor is the omission of CEO compensation data. Given that CEO educational attainment may be correlated with compensation incentives (well-educated CEOs receiving better compensation), \\textit\{section 6.2\} recreates this regression with compensation figures included. Because of the limitations of WRDS-available data, this presents a fundamental tradeoff; the baseline regression omits compensation data but has 340 biotech observations, while the compensation-adjusted regression includes valuable compensation controls but has only 186 biotech observations. Nonetheless, as discussed further in \\textit\{Section 6.2\}, both regressions reveal that biotech-specific companies potentially exhibit stronger performance under CEOs with advanced degrees.\
\
The coefficient demanding higher scrutiny under the Biotech industry umbrella is the undergraduate factor. The undergraduate factor has a high magnitude, negative coefficient significant at the 10\\% confidence level for ROA. However, it is important to contextualize a negative UG Factor coefficient around factor loading and data-specific limitations. To begin, factor loadings (available in \\textit\{Section 3.4\}) reveal that 27\\% of Undergraduate Factor variance can be attributed to the PHD binary variable in the negative direction. While smaller than the impact of the undergraduate binary variable, this still constitutes a significant impact on factor construction in the opposite direction of a variable deemed to have a significant and positive relationship with ROA. Furthermore, given that the majority of sample CEOs have an undergraduate degree, primary factor construction draws from variables for top 20 institutions, meaning the factor loading may have less explanatory power than that of advanced degrees in the dataset.\
\
Within the control set, Biotech demonstrates significant coefficients on the five same variables as the overall panel baseline specification. For all five variables (firm size, equity capital, charter value, retained earnings, R\\&D Intensity), the coefficients tell an equivalent story. R\\&D intensity in particular has a high and drastic negative impact on ROA, likely indicating that Biotech firms investing more heavily in research and development processes are heavily constrained with regards to profitability that is scaled by their assets.\
\
All in all, the positive and significant coefficient on an advanced degree factor (representing PHD attainment) provides initial positive evidence in favor of the relationship between advanced CEO education and high R\\&D firm performance. Robustness checks that include compensation and compensation interaction terms (albeit using a constricted dataset limited by Execucomp data availability) are detailed in \\textit\{Section 6.2\} and \\textit\{Section 6.3\}. \
\
\\subsubsection*\{Semiconductor \\& Hardware and Software Baselines\}\
The Semiconductor \\& Hardware and Software baseline industry-specific regressions provide no significant coefficients for educational attainment. Interestingly, the only significant controls present are the controls for equity capital and charter value, indicating that debt utilization and market premium perceptions are in tandem with ROA. The lack of a significant or even negative coefficient on R\\&D intensity seems to indicate that R\\&D costs are not associated with lower ROA for semiconductor and hardware firms. This is possibly due to the fact that R\\&D expenses are accepted and ingrained components of the hardware product development cycle, and that firms are better adjusted to protecting their earnings from increasing development costs. The Semiconductor and Hardware baseline regression full results are tabled in Appendix K.\
\
For Software companies, baseline industry-specific regression provides no significant coefficients for educational attainment. Charter Value and Retained Earnings are the only positive and significant coefficients that explain ROA. Similarly to semiconductor and hardware firms, there is no significant relationship with R\\&D expenditure. This seems to indicate that the majority of the significance in the overall panel specification can be explained by the heavy impact of R\\&D on biotech ROA performance. The Software baseline regression full results are tabled in Appendix L.\
\
As discussed in \\textit\{Section 2\}'s literature review, CEO compensation historically has demonstrated a significant impact on CEO decision-making and firm outcomes. While preliminary and positive biotech results are promising, the other two industries have potential to demonstrate significant results when CEO compensation incentives are taken into account. \
\
\\subsection\{Compensation-Inclusive Regression\}\
Compensation has a high potential for interrelation with both financial performance (ROA) and CEO educational attainment. Although the relationship of causality is nebulous at best, CEO compensation and education likely go hand-in-hand; well-educated CEOs are qualified for high-paying leaderhsip positions. The effects of CEO compensation on management decision-making, and the trickle-down effects for ROA and other measure of company performance, are well-documented by previous papers tackling the specific issue.\
\
The fundamental issue with the inclusion of CEO compensation in this analysis is the limitation that it places on executing analysis on a sufficient number of samples. After omitting CEO-firm-year observations for which Execucomp did not have available compensation data, the panel datset shrinks from 943 observations to 601 observations. For the industry-specific specifications, biotech sample counts reduce to 186 CEO-firm-year observations, Semiconductor \\& Hardware sample counts reduce to 274 CEO-firm-year observations, and Software sample counts reduce to 141 CEO-firm-year observations.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{Compensation-Inclusive Overall Panel\}\
\
The overall compensation-inclusive regression results are analogous to the baseline overall panel specification. No educational attainment factor is significant at even the 10\\% level. Four of the 11 included controls display statistically significant coefficients, and all four of these controls are financial controls.\
\
It is interesting to note that the MBA Factor has a P-value of 0.109, indicating near-10\\% significance with a relatively large and positive coefficient (largest magnitude out of all 5 education factor coefficients). Given that there are significant results across the different degree types for the remaining sub-industry regressions, this potentially foreshadows an interesting phenomenon; while advanced degrees and different degree types drive factor significance in sub-industry regressions that include compensation, the MBA factor's near-significant coefficient endures throughout the remaining observations in the panel dataset. This potentially suggests that an MBA degree, when contextualized with compensation incentives (option risk and equity compensation valuation) may coincide with slightly stronger CEO-firm performance in ROA. Potentially, the availability of compensation data for all 943 CEO-firm-year observations could reveal stronger statistical significance for the MBA factor. Appendix M provides the full regression results for the Overall compenstaion-inclusive specification.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{Compensation-Inclusive Biotech\}\
\
Applying a compensation-inclusive specification to our biotech subset of firm-year-CEO observations leads to an interesting potential nuance about the type of advanced degree that may benefit biotech CEO-firm combos. Under the reduced number of observations, the PHD factor carries the largest positive coefficient in the compensation-inclusive regression, but with lower statistical significance than the core baseline output. This can likely be attributed to the substantial reduction observations from 340 to 186 firm-year-CEO rows. \
\
The primary takeaway from the Biotech compensation regression is the presence of a significant and negative coefficient on the MD Factor. At the 1\\% significance level, a CEO having an MD has a slight negative impact on firm ROA. Interestingly, this flip from the positive PHD coefficient provides some nuance with regards to the value of advanced degrees for leadership at Biotech firms. It appears that the specific type of advanced degree may lead to different observations of ROA; MD degrees potentially carry less complex relevance for the execution of managerial duties in a biotechnology company. \
\
The MD Factor remains negative in the baseline biotech regression, although not significant. Alongside the reduction of the size of the dataset, this may suggest that the inclusion of compensation controls amplifies the negative value of an MD; once payment of MD-earning CEOs is accounted for, their performance relative to peers becomes lower at a statistically significant level.\
\
Once again, the positive-coefficient MBA Factor edges close to 10\\% significance, indicating a potential slight benefit of an MBA-educated CEO when trying to select a high ROA company. This implies that leveling the compensation playing field leads to slight increased "MBA attainment value" independent of a CEO's paycheck.\
\
Once again, there are five financial controls (firm size, equity capital, charter value, retained earnings, and R\\&D intensity) that all yield statistically significant coefficients. R\\&D intensity continues to demonstrate a high significance, and extreme negative relationship with ROA. Whereas GDP Growth did not have a significant relationship with biotech ROA in the baseline sample, it demonstrates a positive relationship in the compensation-adjusted sample. This is likely because GDP growth effects are absorbed by compensation variables; market up-swings tie directly to better CEO compensation (equities and options), potentially driving the significance of GDP growth in this regression. [TO BE UPDATED, POTENTIAL REGRESSION WITH GDP COMP INTERACTION TERM]. \
\
Appendix N provides a comprehensive regression table capturing coefficients and statistics for the variables of interest in the semiconductor-specific compensation-inclusive specification.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{Compensation-Inclusive Semiconductors \\& Hardware\}\
The inclusion of compensation controls unveils a key finding about Semiconductor and Hardware companies. For educational factors, there are statistically significant coefficients on MBA, PHD, and MD factors.\
\
Factor coefficients for PHDs and MBAs potentially paint a striking story validating the necessity of "integrative complexity" in hardware leadership. While the PHD factor boasts a positive coefficient, the MBA factor has a smaller, negative coefficient. This potentially implies that the management-style leadership of an MBA (that King et al. found to benefit banks throughout robustness checks) is not as compatible with semiconductor leadership. On the other hand, after addressing omitted variable bias in missing compensation data, a PHD corresponds to increases in ROA at a significant level.\
\
This reinforces support for the hypothesis of this paper \'97 that high R\\&D companies benefit from leadership with complex, technical education \'97 by demonstrating a positive relationship between PHD attainment and financial performance (ROA). \
\
The positive coefficient on the MD factor should be heavily scrutinized and not interpreted as a significant result. There are zero semiconductor CEOs in the compensation-inclusive sample that boast an MD degree (as is to be expended in a hardware sub sample). Any significant relationship professed by the MD Factor between MD degree attainment and ROA is attributable to noise from the weighting of other degree types in the construction of the MD Factor (reference Appendix F). \
\
Overall, this constitutes a positive and significant result suggesting that high degree attainment (PHD completion) outperforms management education (MBA completion) as a predictor of firm performance (ROA). Full regression results for the semiconductor-specific compensation-inclusive specification are included in Appendix O.\
\
\\subsubsection*\{Compensation-Inclusive Software: A Cautionary Tale\}\
\
Software results (detailed in Appendix P) for the compensation-inclusive regression do not provide substantive value to the results of this analysis because of data limitations. There are only 19 unique firms and 141 firm-year-CEO observations in the software sample. Furthermore, there exists the continued presence of resultant factor-related noise (i.e. MD Factor significance), meaning that any significant coefficients on educational attainment must be interpreted with extreme caution.\
