\\section\{Conclusion\}\
\
The baseline dataset presented in this paper emulates the work of King et al., constructing 943 firm-year-CEO observations from fiscal years 2015 to 2025. Controlling for firm-specific attributes and CEO-specific personal information, the baseline model provides a statistically significant, positive relationship between CEO PHD attainment and firm ROA. \
\
Expanding the dataset to include CEO compensation controls (total compensation and equity attribution), industry-specific regressions provide refined accounts of degree value in specific sectors. For Biotech, MD degrees are shown to be slightly detrimental, providing added nuance to the aforementioned value of an advance doctorate degree; while industry-relevant PHDs may correlate with improved ROA performance, CEOs with non-relevant medical degrees may associate with slightly detracted firm performance. In the semiconductor sector, the same double-edged sword nuance emerges; statistically significant findings suggest that PHDs are associated with improvements in firm performance, while MBAs are associated with negative outcomes for ROA (when controlling for firm compensation). When leveling the playing field of CEO compensation, degree nuance emerges as the trend; certain kinds of high-complexity degrees improve associated operating performance of CEOs, while others can even detract (for biotech and hardware). For software firms, limited effects emerge across the two core specifications (partially due to data unavailability in the compensation-inclusive software dataset).\
\
The primary limitation of this analysis is endogeneity; there is a well-acknowledged possibility that CEOs with strong education may gravitate to better-performing firms. Although panel fixed effects address time-invariant selection bias, there is an undeniable likelihood that endogeneity will filter through. Nonetheless, the observation \'97 that innovation-driven companies tend to perform better under CEOs with relevant, higher education degrees in hardware and biotech \'97 might motivate additional research in pursuit of an endogeneity solution. Future exploration of this dataset could employ instrumental variables or even the construction of a natural experiment in order to strengthen social science causality.\
